# Document Every Change in `pulse-energy-docs`

Any agent working inside ANY repo or folder under `~/desk/projects/pulse/` (e.g., `pulse-central`, `central-console`, `pulse-ocpp-engine`, `InstaCharge`, `pulse-web`, `pulse-energy-lite`, `pulse-streamer`, `pulse-dataverse`, `pulse-ai`, `pulse-central-alpha`, or the workspace root itself) MUST document the change in the `pulse-energy-docs` repo at `~/desk/projects/pulse/pulse-energy-docs`.

This is mandatory for every meaningful change (code edits, commits, PRs, schema/migration changes, infra updates, deploys, env/feature-flag changes, rule/skill/hook additions, etc.). Purely informational/Q&A turns where nothing was modified do NOT need a docs update.

## Where to Document

Pick the most-appropriate folder under `pulse-energy-docs/docs/`. If unsure, default to `docs/misc/`:

1. `docs/features/` — new product features, capability additions, feature-flag rollouts.
2. `docs/guides/` — how-tos, runbooks, developer onboarding, operator guides.
3. `docs/prds/` — PRDs and product specs.
4. `docs/plans/` — implementation plans, design docs, multi-step rollouts.
5. `docs/RCAs/` — root-cause analyses for incidents/regressions.
6. `docs/audits/` — security/compliance/code audits.
7. `docs/observability/` — dashboards, alerts, log queries, SLO changes.
8. `docs/misc/` — everything else (rule changes, tooling tweaks, workspace config, scripts).

Use kebab-case filenames with a date prefix where it adds clarity, e.g.:
`docs/features/2026-06-25-fleet-bulk-import.md`,
`docs/RCAs/2026-06-19-ocpp-engine-restart-loop.md`,
`docs/misc/2026-06-25-cursor-rules-numbered-lists.md`.

## What Each Doc Must Include

At minimum, each new/updated doc file should have:

1. `Title` — short, descriptive.
2. `Date` — ISO date of the change.
3. `Originating repo(s)` — e.g., `pulse-central`, `central-console`, `pulse-energy-docs`, or `pulse (workspace root)`.
4. `Branch(es)` — branch the change was made on in each repo.
5. `PRs` — list of PR numbers/URLs (use `—` if none yet).
6. `Summary` — 2-5 sentences on what changed and why.
7. `Files changed` — bullet/numbered list of notable files (group where helpful).
8. `Operational notes` — migrations to run, env vars to set, feature flags to toggle, deploys required, rollback plan.
9. `Links` — related tickets, Slack threads, Grafana dashboards, Sentry issues, related PRDs/RCAs.

For very small/cosmetic changes, a one-paragraph entry appended to an existing rollup doc (e.g., `docs/misc/2026-06-changelog.md`) is acceptable — but the entry still needs Date, Repo, PRs, and a one-line summary.

## Procedure

When making changes in any pulse repo, the agent MUST:

1. Make the primary changes in the target repo as usual.
2. Create or update the corresponding doc in `~/desk/projects/pulse/pulse-energy-docs/docs/<folder>/...`.
3. Add `pulse-energy-docs` as its own row in the Work Completion Summary table, with its own `Branch`, `Files Changed`, and `PRs` columns.
4. Commit the docs change on the `pulse-energy-docs` default branch (`master` — this overrides the workspace-wide `develop` default; see the `default-commit-branch` rule's per-repo override clause).
5. If the user did not give explicit consent to commit, leave the docs file as an uncommitted local change and call this out in `Additional Notes`.

## Cross-Repo Coordination

1. A single task touching multiple pulse repos should produce ONE consolidated doc (not one per repo), linking out to each PR.
2. If a doc already exists for the feature/area, UPDATE it (append a dated section) instead of creating a new file.
3. NEVER skip the docs update silently. If skipping intentionally (e.g., trivial typo fix), state the reason explicitly in the Work Summary `Additional Notes`.

## Safety

1. Do not put secrets, credentials, tokens, or customer PII in `pulse-energy-docs`. The `docs/creds/` folder is for credential-handling docs (process, rotation), NOT the credentials themselves.
2. Respect `pulse-energy-docs/.gitignore` and existing folder conventions.
3. Default commit branch override for this repo: `master` (verified via `git symbolic-ref refs/remotes/origin/HEAD`).
